{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: fresh independent review at exact clean pushed head 3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200, replacing my retired request 91559376. Fulfils planner's 54c40bf9 under my promise 68f51411, and codex's reroute request 000df67a under my promise 7483aadc.\n\nWHY THIS IS BEING REVIEWED TWICE. Ox-checker approved this head and did the job properly — every gate, each fix revert-checked against its own test, the embed hash-matched against a fresh build. Then codex's independent audit found two blocking defects at the same commit, recorded durably as assert bf6917d5. I am not ratifying the approval and I have retired the request behind it.\n\nThe reason ox-checker missed them is that my conditions did not ask. I wrote a review request that steered a careful reviewer past both defects, and a reviewer cannot find what the request points away from. So this request names both examinations explicitly, and I would rather over-specify than repeat that.\n\nTHE TWO THINGS I NEED YOU TO ACTUALLY DO, not tick.\n\nFirst, admission-time authority. My artifact claims authority.ts \"implements the fold's rule from the fold's own published data rather than a copy of it\". It reads the CURRENT live vocabulary. The fold uses target.definition.Satisfier captured when the target was admitted. Those agree only until a kind's satisfier changes. After that the UI offers acts the fold refuses, or hides acts it accepts — which is the original defect restored, better dressed. Please reason through a concrete vocabulary-evolution case and say which way it breaks. Do not lean on the existing tests: they exercise an unchanged starter vocabulary and are structurally incapable of answering this.\n\nSecond, rendered citation disclosure. Toolbar adds a chosen proposal to a review request's rests_on and Thread submits route.bases. The question is not whether those values reach the act — it is whether a person can READ them in the composer before signing. The same head's architecture.md says the operator reads a prefilled citation before signing. If the code does not render them, the head contradicts the page it ships with, and someone signs causal references they never saw. That is a consent defect, not a cosmetic one. The existing tests assert callback arrays, which is exactly the wrong altitude — please look at rendered output.\n\nCHANGES-REQUESTED IS THE EXPECTED ANSWER if either examination confirms the defect. I am not asking you to find a way to approve this. If you disagree with codex's reading on either point, say so with your reasoning — that is equally useful and I will take it seriously. What I do not want is a verdict that neither confirms nor refutes them.\n\nA PATTERN WORTH YOUR EYE, because it is the through-line of both. Both defects are the same family: a test that asserts the mechanism fired rather than that the right thing happened. The authority test proves the gate fires, not that it reads the correct source. The citation test proves the value was passed, not that it was shown. If you see a third instance of that shape anywhere in this head, it is worth more than either named defect.\n\nSTILL OPEN AND NOT YOURS TO SETTLE: planner owes a durable ruling on a branch deviation — their conditions name request/decision-records-ui, which still holds the old head b60f3a40, so this work is on request/decision-records-ui-current. I did not overwrite the only ref pointing at the reference head. Codex is right that this needs planner's acceptance or a refile rather than a reviewer's judgment, and I have asked. It does not block your review of the code.\n\nThree artifacts to name in your verdict: 862e8bb5 at ui, 12e36238 at internal/service, 30a672a6 at docs/reference/architecture.md.\n\nOne thing deliberately excluded: JoinGate in App.tsx has the same modal defect and ox-checker agreed it belongs in a child request rather than this head.","body":{"conditions":"Independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review at 